this won't be a spoiler review since I  feel like there isn't a major rush for  people to see this show and the good  word of mouth surrounding Andor is  slowly bringing more attention to it  within andor's praise you have probably  heard that this is the best Star Wars  content in a long time and technically  that is true but to be fair there isn't  a lot of competition the book of Boba  Fett was pretty bad Obi-Wan was a  nightmare behind the scenes and on  screen and the Mandalorian is good at  times but it has its own issues Andor is  a clear standout in quality however  don't go in expecting a magnum opus that  is going to blow your socks off this is  a slow paced show that takes its time  with characters and plot progression and  everything it sets out to achieve is  done superbly and pretty much flawlessly  this shows a spy Thriller with a big  emphasis on drama and with that kind of  emphasis you need to create a colorful  cast of characters ironically out of all  the Star Wars content Andor has the most  interesting Ensemble which is really sad  consider during Obi-Wan just came out  and that show was so dull in terms of  character work for Andor we have Cassian  who is a good POV character for the  audience since he's getting ushered into  this new Rebel world he feels very  compelling because he's so reluctant to  join the cause and is instead swayed by  his own motivations he's not blindly  joining the Rebels for unknown reasons  that the writers don't elaborate on but  he acts realistically to serve himself  this in turn makes the plot a bit  unpredictable because it doesn't go down  that stereotypical path of the  protagonist turning into an immediate  good guy then The Supporting Cast I  think is Rock Solid and there are even  some Game of Thrones alumni who show up  like Lord Royce the wave and qyburn who  all do an excellent job as well as some  other notable supporting characters like  ibon moss and I'm not trying to say his  last name who was amazing in the bear  and Annie circus I adore Andy Circus and  I really hope he gets more live action  work because this man can act and he had  an engrossing mod monologue that's  filled with so much emotion then this  isn't even getting into the best part of  the cast which is Stellan Skarsgard if  this guy chooses a project then I'm  immediately interested in it given that  he's been on a hot streak and if he's  using Andor of all shows that bien then  there has to be a reason he is a  world-class actor and any line of  dialogue he delivers is done with so  much weight and gravitas his character  in the show is by far the most engaging  because he's at the center of the  Rebellion so any plot development around  him is always riveting this also ties  into Mon mothma because she's also at  the center of the Rebellion Big Bang  every scene she is in has so much  tension because she has to act very  carefully so she doesn't set off any red  flags this ties in a more political and  scheming aspect to the show that it kind  of shares with Game of Thrones it  actually makes the conflict of starting  the rebel Network as surprising Delight  because of how difficult it is here  characters don't just snap their fingers  and spawn in allies like in the movies  because that actually takes time and  lots of hard work this is where the meat  of Andor is and since the writing is  really well done it excels in these  dramatic aspects probably because the  show is being passionately made by Tony  Gilroy who also wrote Rogue one in the  Bourne trilogy so the man is goaded this  is not a kiss of Obi-Wan where Disney  forces it into existence it butchers the  original direction of the story to fill  the mini-series formats so the fact that  this show both has a great and solid  direction from a creative leader who is  dedicated to the project and great  actors who want to be in the show speaks  volumes for the quality of Andor along  with the fact that this actually feels  like a feature-length show because  Disney Plus shows have been gaining the  reputation of being like 30 minutes long  once you disregard the intro and outro  Andor has a few episodes of that  duration but most of them are about 40  to 50 minutes long so that's another  sign that the creators are putting care  into the story instead of rushing to a  conclusion there is no filler like the n  endless amount of side quests in the  Mandalorian and half of the book of Boba  Fett being flashbacks anzor follows  different perspectives in this expansive  universe and it all intertwines in a  glorious fashion now going into this  show it starts out pretty slow and I  honestly had a hard time getting through  the first two episodes given that they  had to set up a lot of material like  dozens of characters background and  motivations in cassian's backstory the  plot wasn't that engaging at that point  but it evolves and becomes more  interesting once cassim becomes  entangled with the Rebels the second he  gets into a room with selling  skarsgard's character it becomes really  fun so if you do decide to try the show  at least get to episode 3 because the  first three episodes have one Big Arc  and the other mini plot arcs that  casting goes down get even better also  Ander doesn't appeal to people who want  constant action in lightsaber battles it  has action in small quantity and the  show earns it by properly building it up  and having extremely satisfying payoff  as an example Cassian means a crew of  people and they decided to do a heist  and instead of having a fast pace where  right after meeting them they cut to the  mission casting instead has to learn the  operation and in turn meets the  characters everyone is very fleshed out  because of this and they all have  compelling backstories so when we  inevitably get to the action which is  also really well done from a writing  standpoint and visual standpoint we  actually care about these people another  big aspect of the show in terms of plot  and writing is that there isn't a  constant barrage of terrible mistakes  there's no Leia getting chased Through  the Woods by bounty hunters who can't  catch her or characters surviving  getting skewered by a lightsaber Andor  feels realistic and grounded and there's  nothing in the writing Department that  compromises that feeling because of this  in a more slow pace the show feels  significantly more gritty and foreboding  and since we are slowing down the  writers also give the villains over in  the Empire a lot of screen time at first  I was kind of scared these scenes would  be dull since it's a bunch of old people  talking about Security in politics but  that isn't the case the rendition of the  Empire here is very competent and makes  them seem much more menacing because  they are constantly on the heels of our  heroes and tracking them that's why  these scenes are so engaging because  they are directly tied to the plots  they're setting up new and interesting  characters there's even conflict amongst  the ranks to add even more spice and  it's actively intriguing because we  never seen this kind of meticulous  detail before in Star Wars that's one  major aspect about the show that I love  so much because Star Wars has such a  rich and immersive universe but because  of bad past writers on different  projects they essentially squander the  potential of the IP and it becomes very  Bland as a result Tony Gilroy brings in  that much needed feeling of depth and  fully explores everything tied to the  Rebels the Empire and how each planet is  being impacted thankfully this detail  within the world builds the Empire's  stronger antagonists and doesn't portray  them as buffoons it makes them feel like  a real intangible threat that is  pursuing Our Heroes which thankfully  there isn't annoying amount of  Stormtroopers constantly missing to make  the villain unantagonizing like an  Obi-Wan because when [ __ ] goes down some  characters who come to like will die the  show in that regard isn't afraid of  killing characters and that's refreshing  because it doesn't give off the notion  of plot armor that destroys any  semblance of tension it makes the action  significantly more intense despite us  knowing that Cassian will survive until  the events of Rogue one we don't know  the outcome of different Side characters  and how certain story elements will play  out that's why the show has to establish  likable characters and they do that  expertly going into a more Technical and  production point of view for Andor the  show still doesn't show any flaws for  starters every set piece is really  refreshing and is much needed since  almost every show so far has been said  in the dullness of Tatooine like we have  a mining planet that is being  destabilized by the Empire a more  mountainous planet that looks like the  best parts of Scotland Coruscant is back  and all the Interiors for the Empire are  so eerie and corporate and is so devoid  of Life given that everything is washed  out in white essentially contrasting the  rest of the production design in the  show this was really the first time that  I've been impressed by the sets for  Disney plus Star Wars content and I'm  happy that they are giving the show a  bigger sense of scale than the  Mandalorian as an example if they were  using the volume regularly then they  easily fold me because it looks like  they're using practical locations and  using VFX is a form of set extension I  tend to prefer this more because it  makes the world feel tangible when you  really build it it's infinitely more  impressive to me and is aw inspiring the  volume used well can blend into an  already existing collection of good sets  but when you primarily use a volume it  can become a little noticeable for the  VFX this isn't a show that requires a  lot of it since there isn't a big action  set piece in every episode so it's  primarily used to ground Us in the world  through environments Disney is not  sparing any expense and the VFX is very  immersive because it brings a staggering  amount of quality to the details of each  world and even when there is an emphasis  on V effects like this TIE fighter  flying over ahead it's very realistic  like watch this brief clip  through the interaction with the  environment and the characters being  scared to death and being deafened by  the loud noises establishes this as  being real then when the show decides to  go big with VFX it really blows you away  with the Striking visual design Andor is  just a beautiful show and even the  cinematography doesn't miss a single  beats the show hooks me really early on  with this long take focused on a  close-up of cassian's face as he's being  pestered by two officers it immediately  allowed Diego Luna to impress us with  his great acting and it was a very bold  shot so right away the show establishes  a high bar for cinematography the music  is also a major standout as well this  isn't like Obi-Wan where you probably  have no memory of what the main theme  was because here is very memorable the  composer for Andor is also the same  composer for succession and the main  theme for that show is Iconic so to say  the least he brings just as much talent  to Andor I particularly love the  emphasis being put on violins and it  gives a lot of the music within the  soundtrack such a beautiful and  uplifting endearing feeling it betrays  the notion of Breaking Free of tyranny  and gaining Freedom each episode opens  with a long logo of Andor and honestly I  haven't skipped one because I  immediately am put into a Wonder like  trance by the music that and the  soundtrack goes down some interesting  routes and the composer Nicholas brittle  even tries out a more synth style at  times it's a very refreshing soundtrack  and is pretty close to the Mandalorian  in terms of quality the Mandalorian to  me in terms of music is still my  favorite but that's because Ludwig  Goranson is one of my favorite composers  so yeah everything within the show from  the acting character work writing  different and fun story arcs tone tense  atmosphere and everything on the  production side of the coin is done with  so much expertise to the point that it  deserves to be watched this is a show  made with so much passion and Care put  into the story it may not have  characters people love going in to  immediately sell it but it does a  magnificent job of creating a rich to  roster of characters that you will  easily come to love I was very hesitant  going into the show but I ended up binge  watching it in almost one sitting thank  you for watching and let me know if you  guys have been enjoying Andor

